Given an array arr of n integers, partition the array into two subsets such that the absolute difference between their
sums is minimized.

Example 1:
Input: arr = [1, 7, 14, 5]
Output: 1
Explanation: The array can be partitioned as [1, 7, 5] and [14], with an absolute difference of 1.

Example 2:
Input: arr = [3, 1, 6, 2, 2]
Output: 0
Explanation: The array can be partitioned as [3, 2, 2] and [6, 1], with an absolute difference of 0.

Constraints:
1 ≤ n * sum of array elements ≤ 106
0 < arr[i] <= 104

Approach:
This problem has to be solved in the same way the Bounded Knapsack (0/1) problem is solved. The only thing that needs
identification is that how this comes out to be a 0/1 Knapsack problem as there are no weights or capacity given.
The catch comes from the two subset sums that are involved here. There is no need to find both the subset sums we can use
the total_sum/2 as something that our single subset needs to reach with the condition i.e subset <= total_sum/2. Why
this condition is because anything more than this is already considered for in the other subset. Also, to get the min
difference we have to just manipulate the equation of the condition which comes to -> total_sum/2 - (2 * subset) >= 0.

Naive approach -> TC = O(2^n), SC = O(n) (recursion stack space)
Each call makes up to 2 recursive calls, and every recursive call can goes n levels deep.
Memoization -> TC = O(n*sum), SC = O(n*sum) + O(n) (recursion stack space) = O(n*sum)
Tabulation -> TC = O(n*sum), SC = O(n*sum)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_1 -> TC = O(n*sum), SC = O(2*sum) = O(sum)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_2 -> TC = O(n*sum), SC = O(sum)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_3 -> TC = O(n*sum), SC = O(sum)